1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
 as well, and why every model call should stay inside a step, so Replay never asks the model

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
 again. So I have a blog post as well about reason, you can find it in one of the links

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
 at the end as well. So let's talk about state. We've looked at execution history, that's

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:25,000
 been the entire conversation so far, and that holds until that conversation outlives a single

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:32,000
 execution, or if it moves to another channel. So there are two kinds of state when it comes

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 to these conversational agents. First, what each of these holds. So the durable execution

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 state, it is for recovery, which operations ran, what they returned, which callback is

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
 pending, and agent storage on the other hand is for context, what the customer said and

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 what was agreed upon. And then the scope. Execution state belongs to one execution of

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:05,000
 one function. Agent storage, it belongs to the customer. It is across different executions,

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 across different channels, like I said before, SMS, WhatsApp, voice, whatever that might be,

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
 and it's across agents. And that's the one that makes a phone call, for instance, work as well.

13
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
 And then we have lifetime. When an execution ends, the history within the Lambda console for

14
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 that execution is kept for up to 90 days. It's configurable. But the agent storage, well,

15
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,000
 that lives for as long as you want it to stay. And then who reads it? Well, the runtime reads

16
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 execution state every time we do a replay. And any agent can read agent storage. And that includes us as

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
 humans can read it using our tools as well. So the execution makes sure that we never send

18
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
 the same message twice. But agent storage makes sure that the customer never has to repeat themselves

19
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 because we have that conversational history stored. And that sits in the architecture diagram just like this.

20
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:15,000
 This is DynamoDB. It could be any type of storage as well. We are using DynamoDB. We're keying it to a customer.

21
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:23,000
 In this case, I've been using, there is a storage library for Strands Agent SDK called Strands

22
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
 Agents DynamoDB Storage Library. I think it's version 0.1 so far, so maybe not production ready.

23
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
 But the way it works is a great way to be able to store this type of conversational history across customers.

24
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 So let's just follow this then and add on another channel to it. Because so far we have messages.

25
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
 But what happens when the customer wants to call? Well, let's say then that we have a few beads.

26
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 We're going to follow the yellow here. The call starts. The customer is going to now dial the same number

27
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:06,000
 that they've been texting all along. So we're sending a call incoming to a regular Lambda function.

28
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:13,000
 It is now going to find that customer using the caller ID. And it's completing that pending reply callback

29
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 that we have in the execution history. And it's going to tell us to connect the call to our voice agent

30
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,000
 with the customer ID in the header so we know who the customer is. The voice agent, it runs on target.

31
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:34,000
 And it's going to hold the web socket open for us through the entire call. And it can then read the context

32
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:42,000
 from the agent storage that we had in DiamondDB. So it can open with, well, I see you wanted to move Wednesday's appointment.

33
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:49,000
 Because it knows that from the agent storage. And we're doing then a speech to text, text to speech in this voice relay. And the agents, strands agents on bedrock, we just see it.

34
00:03:49,000 --> 00:04:14,000
 The text turns. So you can use bedrock with that text to be able to give a reply back. None of this touches the durable execution. Checkpointing on every sentence in a voice call would add a lot of latency and honestly wouldn't recover anything.

35
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:25,000
 So while we are waiting, the coordinator is waiting for that call, it isn't running. It is still just suspended as of now.

36
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:32,000
 Same as with that hour-long job that we saw before. Something outside is going to do that callback.

37
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:40,000
 And now it's a phone call. But then the call ends, the customer hangs up. Now the agent is going to write the call summary

38
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 And it's going to complete call ended with whatever was agreed. Moving the appointment to 9am on Wednesday, for instance.

39
00:04:49,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 Now the coordinator, the durable function, is going to resume again. And the specialist can now book it using that summary from the voice call and send out a confirmation text message.

40
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:11,000
 So let's walk through this now. From the beginning. We have the customer phone on the left, agent storage on the right.

41
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:20,000
 The reminder goes out. It's saved as the first turn. It is going to be keyed by that customer so we know who it belongs to.

42
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:31,000
 Can we reschedule? The agent then offers two slots on Thursday. A return goes into the same customer's partition in the agent storage.

43
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:38,000
 Three hours pass. The execution is suspended. Nothing runs. Then easier to just call.

44
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:46,000
 And then they call the number that they've been texting. So switching over to the call instead.

45
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:54,000
 Before the voice agent says a word, the voice agent is going to read the summary from the agent storage so it knows what has happened so far.

46
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
 It's using that customer ID that came in through the call headers.

47
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:08,000
 It opens with the context. You wanted to move Wednesday's appointment. No one has to repeat anything.

48
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:17,000
 The voice agent agrees on 10. It doesn't book. It doesn't have write access. Because that's the booking agent.

49
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:25,000
 The customer hangs up. Call ended completes with what was agreed and the coordinator resumes where it was suspended.

50
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:32,000
 The booking specialist can now book and the confirmation goes out through SMS back in the thread where it started.

51
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,000
 So it's one execution, the text, the call and the follow up.

52
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:44,000
 The customer changes channel. Twice in this case. And the system still knew where the conversation was.

53
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
 What had been done and what it mustn't do again. That's why.

54
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:58,000
 And here's that same conversation. But as a trace this time.

55
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:06,000
 Turn one. The webhook. The coordinator invocation. Routing. The specialist with its bedrock call nested inside.

56
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:10,000
 The send and the wait for the next reply.

57
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,000
 Three hours later a new invocation. And the same trace.

58
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:19,000
 We're using open telemetry. There is a plugin or an SDK for durable functions.

59
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:23,000
 I think it's still in preview. It works really well already.

60
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:29,000
 And it joins it all that way. Using this same conversation ID.

61
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:36,000
 And the voice call. It runs, like we showed, outside of the durable execution.

62
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:42,000
 But it's going to keep that same conversation ID. And meaning that it can join in on that same trace as well.

63
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:47,000
 So the execution history tells you what one execution did.

64
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:52,000
 The trace tells you what happened end to end in this entire conversation.

65
00:07:52,000 --> 00:08:01,000
 And to know whether it's helping customers, you have to record the model, the prompt version, and maybe the validation results for every turn as well.

66
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:07,000
 Like I said, the SDK is still in preview, but it is working really well already.

67
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:13,000
 So let's look just at a few production controls. Things that make this work.

68
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:24,000
 And I think there's six decisions. First off, deploys. An execution is going to be pinned to that specific version that started it.

69
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:29,000
 You know, when you update the Lambda function with new code, it's going to generate a new version.

70
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:36,000
 And the execution is pinned to that. When you invoke it as a version or as an alias.

71
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:45,000
 So you should publish your versions and you shouldn't rename steps while old executions can still receive those.

72
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:52,000
 And there are side effects. Remember to use idempotency keys that I showed in an earlier step as well.

73
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:58,000
 And you should have at most ones for sends, like messages, for instance.

74
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:05,000
 Retrys happen per step. If the execution itself fails, nothing is going to retry it.

75
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:14,000
 So you need to have a dead letter cube in place, for instance, or a status event that can clean up these as well.

76
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:25,000
 And finding the execution. To be able to have a callback through a webhook, for instance, that is able to resume that correct execution,

77
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:34,000
 you need to find that execution id. I tried using APIs for this, but the API to list that,

78
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:40,000
 it defaults to 15 requests per second, which is probably fine in a demo environment.

79
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
 But once you move this to production, that's not going to hold.

80
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:51,000
 So you need to be able to store that, the callback id per conversation, so you can easily list it as well.

81
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:57,000
 The callback API defaults to 6,000 requests per second, which is obviously not better.

82
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:12,000
 Timeouts and double texts, you need to decide what happens when nobody replies, and when a second message arrives mid-turn as well.

83
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:16,000
 And the last one isn't visible for me. Here we go.

84
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:19,000
 So we have the limits as well. Yes.

85
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:27,000
 So 30,000 operations and 100 megabytes of checkpoint data per execution is worth knowing as well.

86
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:39,000
 So they've just increased these limits on the service quite a lot to be able to enable a lot more different use cases.

87
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:49,000
 So there are a few questions I want you to ask yourself when you are deciding a system using durable execution to make sure that you're actually ready for it.

88
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:53,000
 And each one of these is something that I've walked through so far.

89
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:58,000
 So first off, do in-flight executions keep the version they started?

90
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
 And that's invoking a version or an alias.

91
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:05,000
 As I said, it's going to be pinned to a specific version.

92
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:13,000
 Is every model called, generated id, timestamp, uuids, and so on, are they in such a step?

93
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
 And that's the replay rule that I showed early on.

94
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:22,000
 Which of your steps have side effects and what stops them from running twice?

95
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:28,000
 Think about sending message or it could be doing a bank transfer for instance.

96
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:32,000
 Using the idempotency key and at most is important.

97
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:38,000
 And how does a replay find its callback without listing executions?

98
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:43,000
 So you need to store that callback id somewhere when you start waiting for it.

99
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:47,000
 And what happens when a callback never completes?

100
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
 Or if an execution fails entirely?

101
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:55,000
 Think about timeouts, heartbeats, and have something that is able to clean it up as well.

102
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:02,000
 So if you don't have answers to one of these, maybe that's work left to be due before you're able to actually launch it.

103
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:06,000
 If you want to try this, pick one flow that already waits.

104
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:14,000
 Something that you are building that could be a reminder system, an approval thing, or a follow-up of some sort.

105
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:17,000
 And build that using durable functions.

106
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:25,000
 I think we have a lot of stuff already that's either built around queues or state tables and so on.

107
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:31,000
 If you want to try this out, I have some code samples that you can try out on GitHub.

108
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:39,000
 With simple durable execution workflows that you can try both locally and you can deploy them as well.

109
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:47,000
 And if you're building durable functions, do check out the durable viz extension that you can run in your IDE.

110
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:53,000
 It allows you to visualize the workflows in a way that makes it easier to follow along as well.

111
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:59,000
 So, the customer who wrote "Can we reschedule?"

112
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:05,000
 It should never need to know how many Lambda invocations, retries, or channels were behind the answer.

113
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:09,000
 That's the thing that we as developers need to fix.

114
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:13,000
 The execution makes sure that we never send the same message twice.

115
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:17,000
 Agent storage makes sure that the customer never has to repeat themselves.

116
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:23,000
 And the execution history of Lambda Durable Functions can't tell you when every PI was wrong.

117
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:27,000
 So you have to build the checks that make that work.

118
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:35,000
 And with that, I want to thank you all for being in this session and for being here today at serverless days.

119
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
 Thank you so much.

120
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:38,000
 Thank you so much.

121
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:39,000
 Thank you so much.

122
